# Ask the live database, on every pull request and every night, whether the
# security rules the migrations describe are still the rules it enforces.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. Four of those rules already have a check in CI — the
# partner wall (scripts/test_partner_wall.py), the test-login fence
# (scripts/test_sandbox_guard.py), the money doors and the profiles credential
# columns (their migrations) — and every one of them reads the migration
# FILES. A policy edited by hand in the Supabase dashboard, or a migration
# applied by hand and never committed, changes the database and not the files,
# and the file checks stay green. July 2026 had twelve migrations in that
# state. So this asks the database itself.
#
# ON THE PULL REQUEST, not only after the merge. A check that runs post-merge
# can describe a breach; it cannot prevent one. The reviewer sees this beside
# the migration that would cause it.
#
# WHY IT DOES NOT CHECK ROWS. scripts/verify-staging-bays.sh checks rows and
# deliberately runs on a schedule only (verify-warehouse.yml): rows drift with
# no deploy in sight and must not block an unrelated fix. Everything here is
# shape — grants, policies, triggers — which is what a migration changes. The
# walkthrough-videos step below does read rows — published videos, profiles —
# but only to work out both sides of its own comparison from that same live
# snapshot, so a row changing on its own can never turn it red; only a login
# actually seeing more or less than its role allows can.
#
# A MISSING SECRET IS RED. "No invariant was checked" must never render as a
# green tick. The one exception is a pull request from a fork, which cannot
# hold the secret; that skips with a notice.
#
# READ-ONLY. The one statement goes through scripts/pgq.sh, which refuses
# anything that is not a SELECT. The SQL is scripts/invariants.sql, the
# judgement scripts/verify_invariants.py, both under test. The
# walkthrough-videos step below does not go through pgq.sh: each account is
# one batch that opens with `set transaction read only`, sets the caller's
# identity with transaction-local settings, and ends without a commit, so
# Postgres itself refuses any write.

name: Verify security invariants

on:
  pull_request:
  push:
    branches: [master]
  schedule:
    # 08:12 UTC daily, beside the warehouse check at 08:10 and ahead of the
    # backup, so the night's answer is in Slack before the crew's day starts.
    - cron: "12 8 * * *"
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read

concurrency:
  group: verify-invariants-${{ github.ref }}
  cancel-in-progress: false

jobs:
  self-test:
    name: Test the check itself
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"

      # A check nobody has proved can fail is not a check. Offline: hand-built
      # reports for the judge, a stubbed query helper for the wrapper.
      - name: Test the judge
        run: python3 scripts/test_verify_invariants.py

      - name: Test the training-videos judge
        run: python3 scripts/test_verify_app_training_live.py

      - name: Test the wrapper
        run: scripts/verify-invariants.test.sh

  invariants:
    name: The live database still enforces the rules
    needs: [self-test]
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    # A fork cannot hold the secret; everywhere else a missing secret fails.
    env:
      FROM_FORK: ${{ github.event.pull_request.head.repo.fork == true && 'true' || 'false' }}
    steps:
      - name: Checkout
        uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Set up Python
        uses: actions/setup-python@v5
        with:
          python-version: "3.12"

      - name: Ask the live database
        shell: bash
        env:
          SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN }}
          # Production. Named explicitly here for the same reason
          # scripts/pgq.sh refuses to guess one.
          SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF: czprjcskmzzagdztqonm
        run: |
          if [ -z "$SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN" ]; then
            if [ "$FROM_FORK" = "true" ]; then
              echo "::notice::Pull request from a fork: no secret, so the live database was not checked. The file-based checks in CI still ran."
              exit 0
            fi
            echo "::error::SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN is not set, so no invariant was checked. That is a failure, not a pass: add the secret (the same management token deploy-backend.yml uses)."
            exit 1
          fi
          scripts/verify-invariants.sh | tee "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"

      # Rules above; people here. Every account that has not been removed is
      # simulated inside its own read-only transaction and asked which
      # walkthrough videos it can open.
      - name: Check who can watch the Using Forge videos
        shell: bash
        env:
          SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN }}
          SUPABASE_PROJECT_REF: czprjcskmzzagdztqonm
        run: |
          # A fork has no secret; the step above already said so.
          [ -z "$SUPABASE_ACCESS_TOKEN" ] && exit 0
          scripts/verify-app-training-live.sh | tee -a "$GITHUB_STEP_SUMMARY"

  # Push and schedule only. A red pull request is already in front of whoever
  # opened it; a red master or a red night is what nobody is watching.
  notify-failure:
    name: Tell Slack a security rule stopped holding
    needs: [self-test, invariants]
    if: failure() && github.event_name != 'pull_request'
    uses: ./.github/workflows/notify-failure.yml
    with:
      workflow: ${{ github.workflow }}
      cause: >-
        The live database no longer enforces one of the security rules the
        code relies on — a builder login, a QA login or an anonymous caller may
        be able to read or write something they must not.
      detail: >-
        Open the run: the summary names the table and the policy, and says
        which migration to copy the fix from. Until it is fixed, treat the
        partner portal and the QA logins as suspect.
    secrets: inherit
